WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.514
A RAG system that was accurate at launch decays silently.

2
00:00:03.844 --> 00:00:08.423
Freshness, permissions, and provenance are ongoing engineering, not setup steps.

3
00:00:09.223 --> 00:00:12.056
Picture an airline assistant that answers cabin

4
00:00:12.106 --> 00:00:15.061
crew questions from manuals and safety bulletins.

5
00:00:15.391 --> 00:00:17.304
At launch, every answer checks out.

6
00:00:18.104 --> 00:00:21.681
Six months later, it still quotes a retracted safety bulletin.

7
00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:26.057
The bulletin was deleted at the source, but its chunks never left the index.

8
00:00:26.857 --> 00:00:31.137
Nightly full rebuilds don't scale, and leave long windows of stale answers.

9
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:35.270
Instead, catch changes with webhooks or change-data-capture,

10
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.839
and upsert each document by a stable ID.

11
00:00:38.639 --> 00:00:43.389
Deletions are tombstoned, so removed content stops being retrievable immediately.

12
00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:46.520
Every chunk also carries a version and timestamp,

13
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.789
so answers prefer the current revision.

14
00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:52.359
Incident reports are for the safety team only,

15
00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:55.363
so every chunk gets access metadata at ingestion.

16
00:00:55.693 --> 00:00:58.843
The vector search is then limited to what this user may see.

17
00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:04.307
Filtering after retrieval leaks what exists, through rankings and result counts.

18
00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:09.494
Where isolation must be absolute, use separate collections or namespaces per tenant.

19
00:01:10.294 --> 00:01:11.662
Permissions also change:

20
00:01:11.712 --> 00:01:16.153
a purser who left the safety team last week shouldn't still see its reports.

21
00:01:16.483 --> 00:01:18.195
So check access at query time,

22
00:01:18.245 --> 00:01:21.660
against current permissions, not ones baked into the index.

23
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.524
The corpus contradicts itself too: an old rule and its replacement,

24
00:01:26.574 --> 00:01:28.981
a regional variant and a global default.

25
00:01:29.311 --> 00:01:31.450
Metadata like effective date, status,

26
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.765
and authority lets ranking prefer the authoritative one.

27
00:01:35.565 --> 00:01:38.971
The model should surface conflicts rather than silently pick one.

28
00:01:39.301 --> 00:01:43.944
For example: the current rule from June says this, but the superseded one said that.

29
00:01:44.744 --> 00:01:48.235
Incident reports also hold passenger names and phone numbers.

30
00:01:48.565 --> 00:01:50.813
Redact or tokenise them at ingestion,

31
00:01:50.863 --> 00:01:54.105
because anything embedded is effectively distributed.

32
00:01:54.905 --> 00:01:58.311
NeMo Curator detects and redacts personal data at scale.

33
00:01:58.641 --> 00:02:01.069
And if data can't leave your trust boundary,

34
00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:03.604
redact at the boundary or self-host with NIM.

35
00:02:04.404 --> 00:02:05.487
Classic exam trap:

36
00:02:05.537 --> 00:02:10.775
a retracted policy keeps getting cited because the deletion never reached the index.

37
00:02:11.105 --> 00:02:14.831
The fix is deletion propagation, and the lesson below shows how.
